1
00:00:00,014 --> 00:00:03,180
You were by far the most prolific coder at Facebook.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,560
I, like, had never written a single test while working at Facebook.

3
00:00:08,302 --> 00:00:14,820
I don't get a, like, fulfillment talking to AI, but I do feel like the AI is, like, a respected peer now.

4
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:22,100
I'm not so pessimistic, though, that the role of an engineer disappears, because the job of an engineer is to solve problems.

5
00:00:22,440 --> 00:00:24,700
And I think humans are going to have a lot of problems.

6
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:33,130
My guest today is Michael Novatti, co-founder of Formation, longtime Facebook engineer, and the most prolific coder I've ever worked with.

7
00:00:33,190 --> 00:00:35,590
We start with the Summerside project that got him banned for life.

8
00:00:35,690 --> 00:00:38,830
What was going on in his head when he was writing code faster than anyone I knew.

9
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:43,830
Then we get into how he works with AI agents today, including a kind of test I'd never think of writing.

10
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:48,570
And stick around to the end, where I ask whether this is the beginning or the end of engineers.

11
00:00:48,974 --> 00:00:49,710
Let's get into it.

12
00:00:50,030 --> 00:01:00,170
There are many remarkable things I want to talk about, especially things like your 4 million lines of personally edited code this year so far, so I've got a lot of questions.

13
00:01:00,630 --> 00:01:06,970
But I was wondering if I could roll it all the way back to, you know, when we first started working together was Facebook, right?

14
00:01:07,110 --> 00:01:10,350
And you had joined in something like 2009, is that right?

15
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:11,630
Yeah.

16
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:12,390
May 2009.

17
00:01:12,686 --> 00:01:12,990
Yeah.

18
00:01:13,130 --> 00:01:18,980
So can you tell us a few stories from the good old days of Facebook, because you were a legend back there, and I want to hear some of the stories.

19
00:01:19,534 --> 00:01:20,610
I have endless stories.

20
00:01:20,718 --> 00:01:26,350
So let me filter to the non-embarrassing ones, too.

21
00:01:27,510 --> 00:01:29,390
My first day at Facebook, I'm from Canada.

22
00:01:31,190 --> 00:01:33,030
I did some internships in Canada.

23
00:01:33,310 --> 00:01:34,590
It's a very different vibe there.

24
00:01:35,010 --> 00:01:38,890
So I fly down to Silicon Valley, show up on my first day of Facebook.

25
00:01:39,374 --> 00:01:41,950
There's only like 10 people starting that day.

26
00:01:43,342 --> 00:01:44,790
It was like a small conference room.

27
00:01:45,038 --> 00:01:48,450
I think if you get laptops and you set them up and whatever, and then I leave.

28
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:48,950
I'm like, all right.

29
00:01:49,038 --> 00:01:49,550
It's lunchtime.

30
00:01:49,550 --> 00:01:52,970
Let's go like, oh, like you forgot your phone.

31
00:01:53,550 --> 00:01:54,190
I'm like, "Phone?

32
00:01:54,190 --> 00:02:02,870
What phone ?" I get a phone here, like, "Yes, any accessories you need, sir ?" Like, I'm like, "I'm an intern ." And then it's like, I got this fully loaded.

33
00:02:02,870 --> 00:02:06,190
I'm like, it was like a top-of-the-line phone I've never seen before.

34
00:02:06,190 --> 00:02:08,669
I think was a Blackbird, one of the like fully loaded black race.

35
00:02:08,669 --> 00:02:18,350
I was like, "Wow, this is gonna be a different vibe than in Canada ." And at the time, I was gonna go do my PhD at University of Washington in human and computer interaction.

36
00:02:18,530 --> 00:02:21,550
I was like signed up, had my housing, had my everything ready to go.

37
00:02:22,130 --> 00:02:27,670
And that first day really just piqued this like hmm, you know, like I'm gonna give this a shot.

38
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:30,710
And then as you can tell, I never left and I just stayed there.

39
00:02:30,958 --> 00:02:33,830
And was that your first day full-time or were you an intern?

40
00:02:33,910 --> 00:02:36,090
Intern, so I started as an intern in May 2009.

41
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:42,490
And then I was going to do my, I was planning on doing my PhD in the fall, had everything ready to go.

42
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:47,830
And I just kind of dropped out and continuously stayed and converted over a weekend.

43
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,670
Yeah, that's amazing because I didn't know your UW connection.

44
00:02:51,830 --> 00:02:53,710
I still teach there one semester per year.

45
00:02:53,966 --> 00:02:59,930
It's a great university, but you prior to that had also interned at IBM, right?

46
00:02:59,990 --> 00:03:06,730
So can you tell us the difference, the world of difference, I assume, between IBM versus your first day at Facebook?

47
00:03:07,390 --> 00:03:11,950
Yeah, so in Canada, there's much, at the time, there weren't really startups.

48
00:03:12,230 --> 00:03:22,670
Even now, Canada still has a lot of dominant, large institutions that kind of like, there's like five banks and there's like two airlines or three now, or something, maybe four.

49
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:26,110
It's like, you know, the dream companies are the big institutions like IBM.

50
00:03:26,110 --> 00:03:28,290
It was kind of like an interesting place to go.

51
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:30,990
They hired a lot of interns, very different culture.

52
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:35,890
Like, I literally had two commits the entire summer.

53
00:03:36,238 --> 00:03:37,470
What, two commits?

54
00:03:37,934 --> 00:03:43,770
And they were like three lines each, and I was told that I was one of the best interns they've ever had.

55
00:03:43,770 --> 00:03:44,950
And that's an

56
00:03:44,950 --> 00:03:45,810
amazing three lines.

57
00:03:47,086 --> 00:03:57,050
I still remember the one that they found remarkable was, I was working on the just-in-time compiler team for Java, because IBM builds their own version of Java.

58
00:03:57,390 --> 00:03:58,030
I remember that.

59
00:03:58,030 --> 00:04:04,290
It's super controversial, because there's a specification for Java, and then there's the implementation.

60
00:04:05,006 --> 00:04:06,670
They were like, yeah, we're going to build our own Java.

61
00:04:06,730 --> 00:04:09,170
We're going to optimize it for our servers and all this stuff.

62
00:04:09,270 --> 00:04:15,810
So they build their Java for off-the-shelf computers, but also like the IBM platform, PowerPC.

63
00:04:16,878 --> 00:04:19,350
At the time it's like a slightly different hardware platform.

64
00:04:19,930 --> 00:04:37,110
So one of the things, I was like on the PowerPC team and I had to like make some generic Java function header compiled down into like bytecode for PowerPC instead of the other ones because there was like a subtle difference in how something worked.

65
00:04:37,850 --> 00:04:42,430
And I like, it was like literally two lines of code, like switching something, like switching a pointer.

66
00:04:42,430 --> 00:04:48,510
It's kind of like the equivalent of switching a pointer from like a function to like function dot metadata or something like that.

67
00:04:48,510 --> 00:04:50,210
Like, it was just like a very subtle thing.

68
00:04:50,670 --> 00:04:55,190
They were just like, "It's like, wow, you figured this out all on your own ." But what I did do that summer, okay.

69
00:04:55,190 --> 00:05:01,170
This is, hopefully, not —I don't stat —was a statute of limitations on being banned from Google AdSense.

70
00:05:01,170 --> 00:05:03,250
This thing would have been 20 some years ago, right?

71
00:05:03,250 --> 00:05:19,810
So, um, yeah, what I did do that summer, though, because I had so much free time after doing my like one line, two line change and getting applauded, I set up a server at my house that was just like a web server.

72
00:05:20,090 --> 00:05:26,150
I didn't really know how the internet worked but I wanted to set up just my own web server that could be accessible on my internet provider.

73
00:05:26,330 --> 00:05:28,570
And about what year was this in internet history?

74
00:05:29,134 --> 00:05:30,570
2006 I think.

75
00:05:30,910 --> 00:05:30,910
Okay.

76
00:05:31,790 --> 00:05:39,270
So it was definitely doable, but it was like a Pentium 1, 200 megahertz, something like that, 64 megs of RAM.

77
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:43,830
Megs with an M for those of you who haven't seen that before.

78
00:05:46,062 --> 00:05:48,130
So I set up this machine.

79
00:05:48,210 --> 00:05:49,370
I wanted to get email working.

80
00:05:49,650 --> 00:05:51,490
So I have my own domain.

81
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:52,370
I still have it now.

82
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:53,430
And I wanted all my email.

83
00:05:53,610 --> 00:05:54,870
This is a terrible idea now.

84
00:05:55,030 --> 00:05:58,130
Do not do this for security, for all kinds of things.

85
00:05:58,310 --> 00:06:01,590
But Yeah, like forwarding like having email receive on the server.

86
00:06:01,934 --> 00:06:03,530
So, I like learned how all this stuff works.

87
00:06:03,530 --> 00:06:14,450
I learned how, like, a how, like, an email goes from your computer to start with to the internet, through DNS, to the right machines, to like the port, SMTP port on the machine, and all this stuff.

88
00:06:14,450 --> 00:06:23,090
Like, I learned how all this works, like, just like completely organically with, like, poking around and failing and it's like just not working and just like really figuring it out.

89
00:06:23,342 --> 00:06:28,670
And one of the things I did though on this server that got me into a bit of trouble is: there's this TV show, Big Brother.

90
00:06:28,890 --> 00:06:29,270
Oh yeah.

91
00:06:29,550 --> 00:06:30,090
On CBS.

92
00:06:30,478 --> 00:06:31,690
It's like a reality show.

93
00:06:31,950 --> 00:06:33,430
15 people go into a house for a summer.

94
00:06:33,810 --> 00:06:36,050
They can't have any access to the outside world.

95
00:06:36,150 --> 00:06:39,950
They keep voting people off until like one person's left and wins, like a social game.

96
00:06:40,174 --> 00:06:41,030
Really love this show.

97
00:06:41,454 --> 00:06:47,930
And they had this thing where you could like vote for, the public could vote for people who they wanted to like stay each week and it like influenced the show.

98
00:06:48,686 --> 00:06:53,930
And like the voting website, like had no security protections or anything.

99
00:06:53,930 --> 00:07:00,310
Just, it was like a post form with like no, no CSRF.

100
00:07:00,310 --> 00:07:05,770
Like, if you're, you know, web program, like all these things you can do to protect your forms, they did not do.

101
00:07:05,770 --> 00:07:07,930
There was no limits, there was no rate limiting.

102
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:15,910
So, I literally built an auto voter where you can like open like Novatti.ca slash Big Brother or something like that and like choose the person you want to vote for.

103
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:20,510
Just like leave it open and it just kept refreshing over and over and over and it was just like submitting the form.

104
00:07:20,510 --> 00:07:26,630
And like, allegedly, there was like hundreds of billions of votes submitted through this, like I'm running from my home.

105
00:07:28,814 --> 00:07:34,650
What I did learn though, this is what got me into a bit of trouble, is like I had kind of a, I had a few like random websites.

106
00:07:34,750 --> 00:07:38,750
I had a Walt Disney World planning itinerary, like generator thing, like just random stuff.

107
00:07:39,310 --> 00:07:48,770
But we used the same Chrome, like frame around the content just like to reuse it, so the insides were like different apps kind of thing, but the Chrome was the same.

108
00:07:49,102 --> 00:07:55,270
And it had a Google AdSense on it, like a banner ads from Google that I made a little bit of money from.

109
00:07:55,598 --> 00:08:08,730
But the problem with the auto voter is it's like reloading the page constantly, which is like amateur hour for manipulating Google AdSense to try to make money from like page views on your ads.

110
00:08:09,150 --> 00:08:14,230
And they like very quickly permanently banned me in Canada for life.

111
00:08:16,494 --> 00:08:21,670
I love that technology still has bands for life, because it always sounds like this will go on your permanent record.

112
00:08:21,670 --> 00:08:27,550
Like, I was at Microsoft in maybe '98 or something, there was a little game called Midtown Madness.

113
00:08:27,694 --> 00:08:34,830
You drive a little car, and I had sent the team feedback, but I was like full of hubris and like I was just kind of a jerk about it, to be honest.

114
00:08:34,957 --> 00:08:48,590
And so I sent this feedback mail to their email alias to say like, "All these things are wrong with the game ." And they said that that email single-handedly delayed shipping the next product because the team's morale was hit so bad.

115
00:08:48,830 --> 00:08:53,190
And then their general manager said to me, I was banned from Microsoft Games for life.

116
00:08:53,910 --> 00:08:54,350
Wow.

117
00:08:54,590 --> 00:08:55,910
For sending the email?

118
00:08:56,150 --> 00:08:57,330
Yes, totally, man.

119
00:08:57,410 --> 00:09:02,450
I feel like that could be overturned by Steve Ballmer, whoever the person was at the time.

120
00:09:03,630 --> 00:09:04,970
You could get that overturned.

121
00:09:04,970 --> 00:09:07,550
It's probably still on my permanent record here, yeah.

122
00:09:07,770 --> 00:09:10,810
But I wanted to ask about how you got your start in computing?

123
00:09:10,950 --> 00:09:16,250
Because way before we talk about you as a legend at Facebook, I want to start all the way back to when was the first computer?

124
00:09:16,910 --> 00:09:18,710
When did you know you wanted to code?

125
00:09:19,090 --> 00:09:20,100
What was that like?

126
00:09:20,350 --> 00:09:23,630
I think this is a really interesting topic that I think about a lot.

127
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:25,530
Nowadays, it's so different.

128
00:09:25,650 --> 00:09:27,670
People are learning to code earlier.

129
00:09:27,918 --> 00:09:29,630
When I started coding, I was really young.

130
00:09:29,850 --> 00:09:32,290
It was somewhat of an accident.

131
00:09:33,582 --> 00:09:34,980
It started with LEGO Mindstorms.

132
00:09:35,822 --> 00:09:38,910
So that was the first thing where I didn't realize I was programming.

133
00:09:38,910 --> 00:09:39,910
Very similar to today.

134
00:09:39,990 --> 00:09:40,270
Actually.

135
00:09:40,526 --> 00:09:46,788
I was like building a robot for school for a extracurricular class You have to like program a lego mindstorms.

136
00:09:46,850 --> 00:09:49,410
I don't know if they actually discontinued this product now But it

137
00:09:49,410 --> 00:09:51,030
was an amazing product by the way when

138
00:09:51,030 --> 00:09:55,250
they said they were going to discontinue it I also bought one of the remaining sets from lego.com.

139
00:09:55,438 --> 00:09:58,530
I still have it In my closet like perfectly packaged.

140
00:09:58,610 --> 00:09:59,810
You got to keep it in the freezer.

141
00:09:59,950 --> 00:10:10,210
So the colors never change It's like yeah But the mindstorms were amazing because not only like one of the first programmable toys that was truly that way but I was told even the IDE itself was pretty impressive.

142
00:10:10,450 --> 00:10:12,350
Yeah, it did not feel, I didn't know I was programming.

143
00:10:12,750 --> 00:10:21,570
I was just like obsessed with like making this robot follow the piece of tape like on the ground, like a black piece of tape with high contrast against the ground, you have to make it follow the line.

144
00:10:21,870 --> 00:10:23,430
So much fun, I didn't realize it was programming.

145
00:10:23,850 --> 00:10:30,820
Like around the same time, I also really wanted a laptop to carry around at school to take notes for class.

146
00:10:30,910 --> 00:10:33,510
Yes, which for that time was like mind blowing.

147
00:10:33,530 --> 00:10:40,690
There was no smartphones at all, So like there was no like easy way to even record audio within transcribe like nothing It was just like, you know, you wrote your notes on paper.

148
00:10:41,006 --> 00:10:51,030
That was it I was like, I just really want to like type my notes I don't just don't remember why so I saved up like all my paper route money to get this Laptop that was like half broken the battery didn't work.

149
00:10:51,090 --> 00:10:52,130
So it always had to be plugged in

150
00:10:52,130 --> 00:10:54,330
Yes, that's classic and it was probably like seven pounds.

151
00:10:54,574 --> 00:10:55,410
Yeah, very heavy.

152
00:10:55,410 --> 00:11:00,390
So I had this laptop, and then I don't have any idea what prompted me to do this.

153
00:11:00,430 --> 00:11:03,850
But I would go to the bookstore with my mom every like few months.

154
00:11:03,886 --> 00:11:14,090
They had like the clearance sale where they're like trying to like get rid of all these old like programming books that are now obsolete and like just all they're all that like v1 of new edition stuff like that.

155
00:11:14,382 --> 00:11:16,110
And it's like, yeah, I think you pay by the pound or something.

156
00:11:16,302 --> 00:11:19,610
I don't know if you've had this is maybe the Canadian thing I don't know.

157
00:11:19,610 --> 00:11:22,510
Yeah, you like they literally execute you fill up a bag of books.

158
00:11:22,650 --> 00:11:25,130
They weigh it and it's like $5 It's like they're just trying to get rid of them.

159
00:11:25,810 --> 00:11:28,810
So one of the books was like Borland C++ builder

160
00:11:28,810 --> 00:11:30,090
It's classic and

161
00:11:30,090 --> 00:11:31,730
I was like, oh C++.

162
00:11:31,870 --> 00:11:32,930
This looks like interesting.

163
00:11:33,070 --> 00:11:52,810
This is like I don't know what just it's just like I wanted to like try this it comes with a CD-ROM in the back cover He installed Borland C++ builder which at the time I had no idea like even how anything worked Like this was a IDE for writing C++ and I didn't know what Borland was Like I just really did not know anything.

164
00:11:52,890 --> 00:11:54,450
It's like pure like exploration.

165
00:11:55,694 --> 00:12:03,130
Install it, and then I'm like clicking around this interface, figuring out what to do, and I just really organically —I didn't even read the book.

166
00:12:03,150 --> 00:12:07,570
Like, I don't do well with like tutorials for things, like poking, kind of poking around and fiddling with them.

167
00:12:07,790 --> 00:12:11,150
So you're like a tactile learner, like you just, yeah, right in and started typing.

168
00:12:11,214 --> 00:12:16,356
I guess, yeah, I'm like clicking on the interface and like figure out intuitively like how this thing works.

169
00:12:16,782 --> 00:12:18,670
That's why even though I started at an early age.

170
00:12:18,730 --> 00:12:35,010
I think it took me a really long time I would say even like well into my career before I started getting Actually what I would say good at like programming and the concepts because I'm just kind of like intuitively learning all these things and pattern matching and building up this like patina of knowledge of like tactical stuff.

171
00:12:35,170 --> 00:12:35,310
Yeah.

172
00:12:35,630 --> 00:12:39,490
Like the first thing I built was like a GUI builder for forms, it was very business oriented.

173
00:12:39,490 --> 00:12:43,530
So it was like, make like a form with like a name, address, like submit button.

174
00:12:43,650 --> 00:12:44,290
I didn't even know what I was doing.

175
00:12:44,370 --> 00:12:46,730
Just like, it was like poking around, right?

176
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:51,570
And then I like realized that like, you could make like the buttons move like with code.

177
00:12:51,822 --> 00:12:52,770
It makes no sense, right?

178
00:12:52,770 --> 00:12:57,790
But you could have like a submit button, like moving, Like you can control the position of it with code.

179
00:12:58,126 --> 00:13:01,570
When you click one button, you could make another button move five pixels.

180
00:13:02,110 --> 00:13:05,030
Like you can, I was like, I just, this is what I did.

181
00:13:05,326 --> 00:13:15,150
And I just remember from that, I built a Pong game where it was like two buttons, like form submit buttons that would move, that you can move up and down with a keyboard.

182
00:13:15,726 --> 00:13:19,410
And like the ball was like another button that was a square that was moving on a timer.

183
00:13:20,030 --> 00:13:22,350
Yeah, very basic Pong game with like buttons on forms.

184
00:13:22,414 --> 00:13:24,570
That was my first kind of programming thing and

185
00:13:24,570 --> 00:13:28,550
was there anybody in your family that was either into computers or like how did?

186
00:13:28,730 --> 00:13:36,890
this all start because Somewhere I'm missing something where you go in for the pay-per-pound books and you choose Borland C++ Like yeah, it seems surprising.

187
00:13:37,134 --> 00:13:38,810
I always had an interest in computers.

188
00:13:39,350 --> 00:13:51,150
My mom was actually a programmer But like I didn't really know this because she was a kind of stay-at-home mom when I was After I was born so I like I never knew she had a career like a career before right?

189
00:13:51,214 --> 00:13:52,710
And it was like, oh, what did you do?

190
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:54,670
And she was, her title was like analyst at the time.

191
00:13:54,710 --> 00:13:55,650
So she's like, I'm an analyst.

192
00:13:55,770 --> 00:13:56,210
I was an analyst.

193
00:13:56,710 --> 00:13:58,870
And like, I was like, oh wait, but you're actually programming.

194
00:13:58,990 --> 00:14:02,170
It's like, she was a programmer for a like oil and gas company in Canada.

195
00:14:02,770 --> 00:14:05,250
Um, so I think like there's probably some hard wiring.

196
00:14:05,530 --> 00:14:09,270
Um, I wonder if I had today's world, if I wouldn't be a much better programmer now.

197
00:14:09,518 --> 00:14:13,990
I would have been more nurtured and had guidance because I was really all over the place.

198
00:14:14,510 --> 00:14:16,890
But I also wonder, I mean, when it comes to counterfactuals, right.

199
00:14:16,930 --> 00:14:28,810
It's a really interesting question, whether you growing up in a different environment would have bloomed even more versus you giving this tactile learning, direct experience, no guidance.

200
00:14:29,430 --> 00:14:34,190
I wonder if the neurons wired differently when you figured out the whole thing yourself, you know what I mean?

201
00:14:34,250 --> 00:14:44,730
Because part of me feels like maybe you have a deeper understanding at an intuition gut level because you basically had to intuit yourself into this versus going to classes or whatever.

202
00:14:45,550 --> 00:14:46,250
